# qualifier_pat.claim — WHAT A PACKAGE-QUALIFYING VALUE IS SPELLED LIKE.
#
# A regex over the BARE name of a declaration whose value carries the package
# (or an already-package-qualified key). If a resolution site holds one of
# these, the key it builds is not bare and the site is not in the class.
#
# Derived by reading the sites 1daeffa5b / 5bd2724c0 repaired plus the two the
# authoritative-negative round landed (mlir_gen_stmt.cpp:1319, :1366): the
# spellings that actually occur are `pkg`, `sd_pkg`, `dpkg`, `pkg_name`,
# `trait_package`, `qualified_cname`, `qcname`.
#
# ⚠ `qcname`/`qualified_*` are the SECOND-HOP form: the variable is qualified
# because an EARLIER statement built it from a package. The rule follows one
# initialiser hop (pkg_var) and this list catches the rest; a key qualified by
# a hop the rule does not follow lands in UNDECIDED, which is the honest place.
# ⚠ CASE. `kWritStaticPkg` is a package constant and `.*pkg.*` does not match
# it. That one miss put mlir_gen_expr.cpp:6908 in the DECLINES column — a site
# whose very next token is a package literal — and a per-site read is what
# caught it, not the rule. Half of a two-row answer was wrong.
.*[Pp][Kk][Gg].*
.*[Pp]ackage.*
.*[Qq]ualif.*
^q[a-z]*name$
^qc.*
